WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.303
Connection strings, storage keys and other settings often sit in appsettings.json files or environment variables.

00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:13.273
The Azure Key Vault configuration provider reads them from a vault instead.

00:00:13.273 --> 00:00:18.060
In this video, you add it to an Orchard Core host and name your secrets.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.514
A database connection string or a storage key is a secret.

00:00:26.514 --> 00:00:31.391
It is often kept in an appsettings.json file or in an environment variable,

00:00:31.391 --> 00:00:33.920
on every machine that runs the site.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:41.078
The Azure Key Vault configuration provider adds the secrets of a vault to the application configuration.

00:00:41.078 --> 00:00:45.738
Orchard Core reads them the same way as the values of appsettings.json, so

00:00:45.738 --> 00:00:49.000
the settings of its modules can live in the vault.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.223
The provider is a library for the host, not a module:

00:00:53.223 --> 00:00:56.754
you enable nothing in the admin, and it has no screen.

00:00:56.754 --> 00:01:02.228
The Secrets module has its own Azure Key Vault store, for secrets created in the admin.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.170
That is a separate feature.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:14.464
Add a reference to the OrchardCore.Configuration.KeyVault package in your web project.

00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:23.010
Then, in Program.cs, call AddOrchardCoreAzureKeyVault on the configuration of the builder, before the application is built.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.919
The method also extends the host builder and the web host builder, so

00:01:27.919 --> 00:01:31.246
it fits the Generic Host that the documentation shows.

00:01:31.246 --> 00:01:34.130
All three accept an optional token credential.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.592
The vault is configured under OrchardCore, KeyVault, Azure.

00:01:42.592 --> 00:01:47.706
KeyVaultName is the name of the vault, and VaultURI is its full address.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:51.050
When you set both, VaultURI takes precedence.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.156
On the configuration of the builder, the call reads these settings from the

00:01:56.156 --> 00:02:02.017
sources registered before it, such as appsettings.json or environment variables, and not from a tenant.

00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:07.180
With neither a name nor an address, it throws an exception that names the missing setting.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.899
ReloadInterval is the number of seconds to wait between attempts at polling the vault for changes.

00:02:13.899 --> 00:02:16.310
Leave it empty to disable reloading.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:22.682
The provider is added where you call the method, after the sources registered before it.

00:02:22.682 --> 00:02:27.646
A value in the vault replaces the same key in the project's appsettings.json.

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:32.730
How Orchard Core combines the other sources is the subject of a separate video.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:43.689
Called without an argument, the method creates a DefaultAzureCredential, with interactive credentials included.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:49.870
The documentation describes it as appropriate for most scenarios where the application runs in Azure.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.632
To use another credential, pass a TokenCredential to the method.

00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:59.102
The identity behind it must be allowed to read the secrets of the vault.

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:05.520
On a development machine, the documentation describes signing in from Visual Studio or Visual Studio Code.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.462
Azure Key Vault does not allow a colon or an underscore in the

00:03:13.462 --> 00:03:16.763
name of a secret, so the provider translates the name.

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:20.780
Two hyphens become a colon, and three hyphens become an underscore.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.230
The first name on screen is the documentation's example:

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:29.944
its parts, joined by two hyphens, become the path of a configuration key.

00:03:29.944 --> 00:03:34.420
Three hyphens give an underscore, and a single hyphen stays a hyphen.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:40.267
Without a tenant name, the value applies to every tenant, as in the first name.

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:45.843
With a tenant name after OrchardCore, as in the second, it applies to that tenant only.

00:03:45.843 --> 00:03:50.460
This follows the layout of the OrchardCore section of appsettings.json.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.333
In short, create the secrets in the vault, with hyphens in their names.

00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:04.856
Reference the package, call the method in the host, and set KeyVaultName or VaultURI.

00:04:04.856 --> 00:04:08.680
And let the identity that runs the site read the secrets.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.446
The Azure Key Vault configuration provider keeps your settings out of your files.

00:04:15.446 --> 00:04:16.870
Thanks for watching.

